-- title:
--   Divisibility dichotomy modulo a component through a supersingular point
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $5 \le q$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number not divisible by $q$, and $\ell$ is a prime with $3 \le \ell$, $\ell \neq q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$. Further, $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\xi \in L$ is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and `hι` asserts the existence of a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. The field $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$, and `hK` requires $K$ to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79): that is, $K$ is generated over $L$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ by the coefficientwise images under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the field of $\mathbb{Q}$-rational $q$-expansions attached to the congruence subgroup [`CohCarrier.GammaH`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) of level $N_0 = (q\ell)^2M'$ and subgroup $H =$ `levelH (q*ℓ) M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   The ring $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation ring with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field, such that $q$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$; $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and its Laurent series is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ with coefficients pushed into $L$. The element $\varpi \in A$ generates the maximal ideal, and $\varpi_t :=$ `ϖt` satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$.
--
--   Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin A ↥K j` for $j$ regarded as an element of $C$. The ideal $y \subseteq C$ is maximal and contains the image of $\varpi$, and the hypothesis `hss` states supersingularity of the $j$-invariant at $y$: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` (the Drinfeld chart witness, in universally quantified form) concerns the scheme [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout of the two chart morphisms from the middle chart to $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the $j$-finite and $j^{-1}$-finite chart algebras, together with its structure morphism `toBase` to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ and the canonical morphism `ιFin` from $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ into the model. It asserts: for every point $z$ of the model such that the germ at $z$ of the global section coming from $\varpi$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and every point $y'$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ with `ιFin` mapping $y'$ to $z$ and with the supersingularity condition `hss'` at $y'$ (same form as `hss`, for $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$), there exist a complete discrete valuation ring, a ring homomorphism $\sigma$ from $A$ to it carrying $\varpi$ to a uniformiser, power series $f,u,v$ in two variables with $u,v$ units and $f - (X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1) \in (X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the form $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ being [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2)/\big(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v - fu\big)$, subject to the same seven riders (1)–(7) listed below for the instantiated witness.
--
--   The theorem then fixes such data: a point $z$, the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ in the stalk at $z$ with $\varpi_z$ in the maximal ideal, a point $y'$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ above $z$ with the supersingularity condition `hss'`, and `hy'y : y'.asIdeal = y`; together with a concrete witness: a complete discrete valuation ring $W_1$, a homomorphism $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ with $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$, power series $f_1,u_1,v_1$ with $u_1,v_1$ units and $f_1 - (X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1) \in (X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and an isomorphism $e_1$ from the adic completion of the stalk onto $S := \mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2, W_1)/\big(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1\big)$. Writing $\mathrm{STK}$ for the stalk at $z$, $\mathrm{CMP}$ for its adic completion at the maximal ideal, $\mathrm{toC} : \mathrm{STK} \to \mathrm{CMP}$ for the canonical map, $\mathrm{mkS}$ for the quotient map onto $S$, and $\mathrm{germY} : C \to \mathrm{STK}$ for the map obtained by identifying $C$ with the sections of the model over the open image of the $j$-finite chart and taking germs at $z$, the hypothesis `hW₁` is the conjunction of:
--
--   (1) for all $a \in A$, $e_1$ takes the image in $\mathrm{CMP}$ of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (via `toBase`) to the class of $C(\sigma_1 a)$;
--
--   (2) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ which is a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ in the sense of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — namely, for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ on `GammaH ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M')` with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$, with the series of $p_g$ nonzero, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent series is the image of $p_f/p_g$, and every embedding $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$, one has $\iota(\tau x) \cdot q\text{-}\mathrm{exp}(g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1}) = q\text{-}\mathrm{exp}(f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1})$, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1}$ is the conjugate of $\gamma^{-1}$ by $\mathrm{diag}(1,q\ell)$ — the automorphism $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$;
--
--   (3) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M') \cap \Gamma(\ell)$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, the induced map on $C$ satisfies $\tau(a) - a \in y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ for all $a \in C$;
--
--   (4) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ and inducing the identity modulo $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W_1$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{M}_2(W_1)$ such that $\theta \circ e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY} = e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY} \circ \tau|_C$, $\theta$ fixes all classes of constants $C(w)$, $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}\,X_j) - \mathrm{mkS}\big(\sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i\big)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_0, \mathrm{mkS}\,X_1$, $c^{q+1} - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, $M_{ij} - c\,\gamma_{ij} \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ for all $i,j$, $c - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$, and $c - 1 \notin \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$;
--
--   (5) for all integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and all primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$ such that each $P_i$ omits $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_0$ or $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_1$, each contains $\mathrm{mkS}\,C(\sigma_1\varpi)$, and each has the tangent direction $(a_i : b_i)$ in the sense that $\mathrm{mkS}\big(C(a_i)X_0 + C(b_i)X_1 + h\big) \in P_i$ for some $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$: if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to the stalk along $e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC}$ are distinct;
--
--   (6) for every prime $P$ of $S$ omitting $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_0$ or $\mathrm{mkS}\,X_1$, containing $\mathrm{mkS}\,C(\sigma_1\varpi)$, and containing $\mathrm{mkS}\big(C(1)X_0 + C(0)X_1 + h\big)$ for some $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, and every $a \in C$: $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}\,a)$ lies in the contraction of $P$ along $e_1$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$;
--
--   (7) [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) — the Laurent series obtained from the $q$-expansion of $j$ over $L$ by multiplying all exponents by $q\ell$ — lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with this element minus $a_0$ in $y'.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that, firstly, for all $a,b \in W_1$ not both in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$ with $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_1}$, the value $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \mathrm{coeff}_{X_0^iX_1^{e_0-i}}(h)\,a^i b^{e_0-i}$ of the degree-$e_0$ form of $h$ is a unit, and secondly, $e_1$ carries $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\,\cdot\,))$ of that element minus $a_0$ to $\mathrm{mkS}\,h$.
--
--   Next, $J$ is an ideal of $C$ and `hJ` requires $J$ to be the infimum of the set of ideals of the form $\big(\tau|_C\big)^{-1}\Big(\big(e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}\big)^{-1}\big(\mathrm{mkS}\,C(\sigma_1\varpi_t),\ \mathrm{mkS}\,X_0,\ \mathrm{mkS}\,X_1\big)\Big)$, taken over all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$. The subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ is required by `hB` to be the $A$-subalgebra underlying the $C$-algebra generated by $\{x \in K : x\varpi_t \in J\}$, i.e. $B = C[J/\varpi_t]$. Finally $W$ is a valuation subring of $K$ with $B \subseteq W$ (`hBW`), and four further hypothesis groups are imposed.
--
--   `hR1`: $C \subseteq B$, and every $f \in K$ is a fraction $g/h$ with $g,h \in B$, $h \neq 0$.
--
--   `hR2`: $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$, and $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$.
--
--   `hR3` (five clauses): an element of $L$ lies in $W$ if and only if it comes from $A$; the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$; $W$ is a discrete valuation ring; an element $b \in C$ lies in $y$ if and only if it lies in $W$ and in the maximal ideal of $W$; and $f \in K$ lies in $W$ if and only if $f = g/h$ with $g,h \in B$ and $h$ a unit of $W$.
--
--   `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here): for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) (the quotient of the polynomial ring in two variables by `drinfeldPoly q k - 1`) whose kernel consists of the elements of $B$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W$, which is compatible with $A \to k$, and which is equivariant for the level automorphisms: for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ with $W$ stable under $\tau$, there is a unit $c$ of $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho \circ \tau = \mathrm{hAction}\,q\,k\,(\overline{\gamma},c) \circ \rho$ on $B$, and $c \neq 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; moreover $B$ is stable under all these $\tau$; every prime $Q$ of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached by some $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and associated $\tau$, in the sense that $\tau$ carries all elements of $B$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W$ into $Q$; an element of $B$ all of whose images under such $\tau$ lie in the maximal ideal of $W$ is divisible by $\varpi$ in $B$; and if $\tau$ (attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$) preserves $y$ on $C$, then $W$ is stable under $\tau$.
--
--   With these data, let $\mathfrak{p}$ be a prime ideal of $C$ containing the image of $\varpi$, with $\mathfrak{p} \subseteq y$ and $\mathfrak{p} \neq y$.
--
--   Conclusion: for all $c_1, c_2 \in C$ there exist $d, s \in C$ with $s \notin y$ such that $s c_1 - d c_2 \in \mathfrak{p}$ or $s c_2 - d c_1 \in \mathfrak{p}$.
--
--   The conclusion is the elementwise form of the assertion that the local ring at $y$ of the quotient $C/\mathfrak{p}$ by a component $\mathfrak{p}$ of the special fibre through the supersingular point $y$ is a valuation ring of its fraction field, the regularity of the Igusa branches through a supersingular point in the sense of Katz–Mazur. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.end_blowupChart_eq_of_on_same_component_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.end_blowupChart_eq_of_on_same_component_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked), where the resulting divisibility dichotomy makes two ends lying on one component comparable, hence equal.
